<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Pranala-Header — the bearing a carrier declares about itself

`<<^ code="&#x0001;" from="?" -> to="uri">>` stands on a carrier's head and declares the address it holds. The
`?` token means ''this carrier itself'' — a self-reference no sigil keyword can express, since a
keyword names a thing and this one names the speaker.

The bearing reads off the SOH frame scan (the deserializer's head recogniser).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/ooda-ha>>

✶ sense the `?` token — a self-reference; the carrier declares its own address, never an edge outward
⏿ orient: the bearing rides the SOH control sigil, once per carrier, ahead of the meta block
◇ the uri names this carrier's canonical `lar:` address; every `?` in the carrier resolves to it
▶ read the bearing off the head; set the self-reference for all sigils in this carrier
↺ confirm exactly one bearing per carrier and a `uri-path` that agrees with it

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! Law (Kānāwai)

''The bearing rides the CONTROL head.'' A carrier declares its own address exactly once, on its SOH
sigil, ahead of the meta block:

A bearing MUST appear exactly once per carrier, on the SOH control sigil, before the meta TOML block.
A bearing MUST carry a `lar:///` URI.
A bearing's URI MUST match the carrier's `uri-path` meta field.
A bearing MUST NOT appear inside an ahu slot body, and MUST NOT repeat in the body on any head.
The `?` token stays reserved — MUST NOT be used as a sigil name elsewhere.

''Why the control head.'' A frame mark states what the reader must do before it can read anything; a
speaking sigil states what a carrier says. The bearing stands first among the former — a reader that
cannot find the address cannot resolve a single `?` in the body — so it rides the head that carries the
frame (`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/memetic-wikitext-framing#/frame-head-lock`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/syntax>>

!! Syntax

```text
<<^ code="&#x0001;" from="?" -> to="lar:///ha.ka.ba/path/to/this-carrier">>
<<^ code="&#x0001;" namespace="⊙" from="?" -> to="lar:///ha.ka.ba/path/to/this-carrier">>
```

The bearing reads off the SOH scan, which anchors on the control code and admits the namespace slot
ahead of it. ''The tail must tolerate the arrow'': `? ->` carries a `>`, so a scan closing on `[^>\n]*`
stops at the bearing itself and finds no head at all. Every scan in this grammar spells it
`(?:[^>\n]|->)*` for that reason.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/schema>>

!! Schema (machine-readable)

''The bearing reads off the frame, never off a sigil pattern.'' The SOH scan finds the head, and the
bearing follows its `? ->`. No pattern here holds the source of truth for that — `frame-marks.ts`
carries the control set, and each reader keeps the scan its own context earned (a prefix stopping at
`&` so an entity-written namespace never reads as the control code; a tail admitting `->`).

```toml
name           = "pranala-header"
kind           = "bearing"
rides          = "SOH control sigil"       # &#x0001; / &#x0011;
self-token     = "?"
once-per       = "carrier"
agrees-with    = "uri-path"

```

<<~/ahu>>

